WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.105
The Cyber Kill Chain is a defensive model for thinking about how cyber intrusions

00:00:04.105 --> 00:00:04.950
progress.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:08.470
Lockheed Martin developed the framework as part of an Intelligence Driven Defense approach

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:11.120
for identifying and preventing intrusion activity.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:14.995
The model describes a sequence of things an adversary would need to accomplish to reach an

00:00:14.995 --> 00:00:15.360
objective:

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.120
reconnaissance, weaponization, delivery, exploitation, installation, command and control,

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.560
and actions on objectives.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.080
For defenders, the value is not the diagram itself.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.360
The value is the set of questions it creates.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:28.600
What stage does the evidence actually support?

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:30.840
Where could the activity have been detected earlier?

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.660
Which control might deny, disrupt, degrade, deceive, or contain the behavior?

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:37.520
What telemetry would confirm or challenge the current assessment?

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.590
The Cyber Kill Chain gives analysts, responders, engineers,

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:44.260
and leaders a shared vocabulary for discussing intrusion progress and defensive

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:45.080
opportunities.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:49.080
It should be used with evidence, threat intelligence, business context, and judgment.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.625
Not every incident follows a clean sequence, and some activity may be compressed,

00:00:52.625 --> 00:00:53.410
repeated,

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:55.856
automated, or invisible to the available logs.

00:00:55.856 --> 00:00:59.435
Used well, the model helps teams organize evidence, improve detections,

00:00:59.435 --> 00:01:02.735
prioritize controls, and communicate risk without turning analysis into a

00:01:02.735 --> 00:01:03.595
rigid checklist.

00:01:03.595 --> 00:01:07.880
A useful way to introduce the model to a team is to treat each stage as a chance to ask

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:10.000
what the defender can see and influence.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.270
Earlier disruption usually reduces later impact,

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:14.900
but late-stage evidence can still be critical for recovery,

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:16.800
legal review, and control improvement.
